en graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(0,1)$ and  $(1,2).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals\n\n$$\n(\\,0,1\\,)\\,\\text{and}\\,(\\,1,2\\,).\n$$" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356503 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356504 13194139535589 true] [17592186356503 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535589 true] [17592186356503 1041 "field-1" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356503 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356504 13194139535589 true] [17592186356503 1110 #uuid "d4dc8f1b-3600-59ca-9f0e-bc7d6ed11f54" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356504 1034 "0" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535589 true] [17592186356504 1111 #uuid "d9ea2f30-cd83-5d8a-b908-b17291f4f101" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356506 13194139535589 true] [17592186356505 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535589 true] [17592186356505 1041 "field-2" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356505 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356506 13194139535589 true] [17592186356505 1110 #uuid "a61a2655-b56d-5549-9963-28fa87d13bf0" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356506 1034 "1" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535589 true] [17592186356506 1111 #uuid "0430e05f-f020-5ee7-b611-6f83ba763a7a" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356507 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356508 13194139535589 true] [17592186356507 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535589 true] [17592186356507 1041 "field-3" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356507 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356508 13194139535589 true] [17592186356507 1110 #uuid "016f4961-f2d0-5c95-a9bd-53294591685d" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356508 1034 "2" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535589 true] [17592186356508 1111 #uuid "c9d1bef4-a62e-5fdd-86a6-c996ccad2a79" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356510 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1089 "q-79041" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1113 #uuid "8bd473db-7b51-5cec-b077-da970bb7b859" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356509 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12x-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =12(1-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12(1+x)(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(1+x)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-1) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)<0$ when $-1<x<1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)>0,$ which has the solutions $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356511 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356512 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356513 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356514 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356515 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1041 "selection" 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356512 13194139535589 true] [17592186356510 1110 #uuid "53b2e87f-9136-55d0-8549-3fbe6afd36d8" 13194139535589 true] [175